  "source_questions": {
    "questions": [
      {
        "source_id": "1",
        "source_raw": "The raven is sent from the Castle Black, which is at the origin, observing the white\nwalkers at the coordinates 𝑟⃗ = 3𝑖̂+6𝑗̂ at the beginning of season 4, where length\n4\nunits are in kilofurlongs. Which of the following statements is correct?\nA. The distance between the white walkers and the Castle Black is 9 kilofurlongs.\nB. The distance between the white walkers and the Castle Black is 6 kilofurlongs.\nC. The distance between the white walkers and the Castle Black is 3 kiolofurlongs.\nD. None of the above is correct.",
        "stem": "The raven is sent from the Castle Black, which is at the origin, observing the white walkers at the coordinates \\vec{r} = 3\\hat{\\imath}+6\\hat{\\jmath} at the beginning of season 4, where length units are in kilofurlongs. Which of the following statements is correct?",
        "options": [
          {
            "key": "A",
            "text": "The distance between the white walkers and the Castle Black is 9 kilofurlongs.",
            "is_correct": false,
            "explanation": "This incorrectly treats the distance as the simple sum of components (3+6) instead of using the Euclidean distance formula."
          },
          {
            "key": "B",
            "text": "The distance between the white walkers and the Castle Black is 6 kilofurlongs.",
            "is_correct": false,
            "explanation": "This confuses one coordinate component (the y-component is 6) with the actual distance from the origin."
          },
          {
            "key": "C",
            "text": "The distance between the white walkers and the Castle Black is 3 kiolofurlongs.",
            "is_correct": false,
            "explanation": "This confuses one coordinate component (the x-component is 3) with the actual distance from the origin."
          },
          {
            "key": "D",
            "text": "None of the above is correct.",
            "is_correct": true,
            "explanation": "The distance from the origin to \\(\\vec r=3\\hat\\imath+6\\hat\\jmath\\) is found by \\(r=\\sqrt{x^2+y^2}=\\sqrt{3^2+6^2}=\\sqrt{45}\\approx 6.71\\) kilofurlongs. Since 6.71 is not equal to 9, 6, or 3, none of A–C is correct."
          }
        ],
        "has_explicit_answer": false,
        "difficulty": "",
        "question_type": "multiple_choice",
        "theme": "physics_abstract",
        "topic_tags": []
      },
      {
        "source_id": "2",
        "source_raw": "If the displacement of an object is given by the function 𝑠⃗ = ((1.3𝑚/𝑠3)𝑡3 +\n(2.7𝑚/𝑠2)𝑡2)𝑖̂, which of the following statement is correct?\nA. This is a constant acceleration motion.\nB. The magnitude of the acceleration is 2.7 m/s2\nC. The magnitude of the acceleration at t = 0 is 0 m/s2\nD. The magnitude of the acceleration at t = 0s is 5.4 m/s2",
        "stem": "If the displacement of an object is given by the function \\vec{s} = ((1.3m/s^3)t^3 + (2.7m/s^2)t^2)\\hat{\\imath}, which of the following statement is correct?",
        "options": [
          {
            "key": "A",
            "text": "This is a constant acceleration motion.",
            "is_correct": false,
            "explanation": "Differentiating shows the acceleration depends on time, so it is not constant-acceleration motion."
          },
          {
            "key": "B",
            "text": "The magnitude of the acceleration is 2.7 m/s^2",
            "is_correct": false,
            "explanation": "2.7 m/s^2 is a coefficient in the displacement term, not the (time-dependent) acceleration value."
          },
          {
            "key": "C",
            "text": "The magnitude of the acceleration at t = 0 is 0 m/s^2",
            "is_correct": false,
            "explanation": "After differentiating twice, the acceleration has a nonzero constant term, so \\(a(0)\\neq 0\\)."
          },
          {
            "key": "D",
            "text": "The magnitude of the acceleration at t = 0s is 5.4 m/s^2",
            "is_correct": true,
            "explanation": "Velocity is \\(\\vec v=\\frac{d\\vec s}{dt}=(3\\cdot 1.3\\,t^2+2\\cdot 2.7\\,t)\\hat\\imath=(3.9t^2+5.4t)\\hat\\imath\\). Acceleration is \\(\\vec a=\\frac{d\\vec v}{dt}=(7.8t+5.4)\\hat\\imath\\), so at \\(t=0\\), \\(|\\vec a|=5.4\\,\\text{m/s}^2\\)."
          }
        ],
        "has_explicit_answer": false,
        "difficulty": "",
        "question_type": "multiple_choice",
        "theme": "physics_abstract",
        "topic_tags": []
      },
      {
        "source_id": "3",
        "source_raw": "An object takes 3 second to move from position A (𝑟⃗ = −4.0𝑖̂+10𝑗)̂ to position B\n𝐴\n(𝑟⃗ = 2.0𝑖̂+5.0𝑗)̂ , where length units are in meters. Which of the following\n𝐵\nstatements is correct?\nA. The moving distance is 7.8 m.\nB. The speed at t = 0 must be 2.6 m/s.\nC. The acceleration at t = 0 must be 0.87 m/s2\nD. None of the above is correct.",
        "stem": "An object takes 3 second to move from position A (\\vec{r} = -4.0\\hat{\\imath}+10\\hat{\\jmath}) to position B (\\vec{r} = 2.0\\hat{\\imath}+5.0\\hat{\\jmath}) , where length units are in meters. Which of the following statements is correct?",
        "options": [
          {
            "key": "A",
            "text": "The moving distance is 7.8 m.",
            "is_correct": true,
            "explanation": "The displacement from A to B is \\(\\Delta \\vec r = (2-(-4))\\hat\\imath + (5-10)\\hat\\jmath = 6\\hat\\imath-5\\hat\\jmath\\). Its magnitude is \\(|\\Delta \\vec r|=\\sqrt{6^2+(-5)^2}=\\sqrt{61}\\approx 7.81\\,\\text{m}\\), which rounds to 7.8 m."
          },
          {
            "key": "B",
            "text": "The speed at t = 0 must be 2.6 m/s.",
            "is_correct": false,
            "explanation": "Knowing only initial and final positions and the travel time does not uniquely determine the initial speed; many motions could connect the points in 3 s."
          },
          {
            "key": "C",
            "text": "The acceleration at t = 0 must be 0.87 m/s^2",
            "is_correct": false,
            "explanation": "The acceleration at \\(t=0\\) cannot be determined from just endpoint positions and elapsed time without additional kinematic information (e.g., constant acceleration plus velocity data)."
          },
          {
            "key": "D",
            "text": "None of the above is correct.",
            "is_correct": false,
            "explanation": "Option A is correct because the straight-line separation between the two position vectors is \\(\\sqrt{61}\\approx 7.8\\,\\text{m}\\)."
          }
        ],
        "has_explicit_answer": false,
        "difficulty": "",
        "question_type": "multiple_choice",
        "theme": "physics_abstract",
        "topic_tags": []
      }
    ]
  },
  "requirements": {
    "new_questions_per_source": 1,
